//? Title: Brent Cycle Detection: Doubling Blocks, Period and Entry
//? Subtitle: Actual checkpoints, exact transition counts and the decimal happy-number map
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A deterministic finite-state iteration eventually has a transient tail followed
by a cycle. Brent's method finds their lengths using constant auxiliary storage.
We begin with the original seven-state map, inspect checkpoints recorded by the
same loop as ordinary detection, and recover the actual cycle entry. A decimal
digit-square application retains the original happy-number examples. Controlled
tails then test exact transition-call formulas for Brent and Floyd. All maps,
orbit prefixes, diagnostic records and digit operations come from native C++.
)

\toc

> A seven-state map: three transient states and a four-cycle

Start at zero and follow 0→1→2→3→4→5→6→3. The first three states are
transient; the remaining four repeat. Thus the least entry index is mu=3,
the least positive period is lambda=4, and the entry state is three.
The native rho-map generator recreates the original successor table exactly.
Blue nodes precede entry; green nodes lie on the cycle.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/brent_functional_graph.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto next=dm::algorithms::rho_successors(3,4);
  const dm::algorithms::CycleFunction f=[&](std::uint64_t x){return next.at(x);};
  const auto work=dm::algorithms::brent_cycle_work(0,f);
  const auto states=dm::algorithms::iterate_states(0,f,next.size());
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> position{{0,0},{2.7,0},{5.4,0},{8.1,0},{10.8,1.8},{13.5,0},{10.8,-1.8}};
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<states.size();++i) {
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=1.4;style.height=1;
    style.fill=i<work.result.mu?dm::RGB{219,234,254}:dm::RGB{220,252,231};
    diagram.node(std::format("s{}",states[i]),position[i],std::format("{}",states[i]),style);
  }
  for (std::size_t state=0;state<next.size();++state)
    diagram.edge(std::format("s{}",state),std::format("s{}",next[state]));
  diagram.text({5.4,2.6},"0, 1, 2: transient tail; 3, 4, 5, 6: repeating cycle")
      .text({6.75,-3},std::format("mu = {}; lambda = {}; entry = {}",work.result.mu,work.result.lambda,work.entry))
      .save_svg("build/brent_functional_graph.svg");
  std::print("Successor table: {}.\n\nNative result: mu={}, lambda={}, entry={}.\n",
      next,work.result.mu,work.result.lambda,work.entry);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Successor table: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 3].

Native result: mu=3, lambda=4, entry=3.
\image(figures/brent_functional_graph.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The successor table is indexed by state label. The returned entry is obtained during native entry recovery, without replaying the tail.)
\alttext(States zero, one and two form a blue chain leading to green state three. Green states three, four, five and six form a directed four-cycle.)

> What doubles, and where the reference actually sits

Write \(x_i=f^i(x_0)\). A finite deterministic map has least integers mu≥0 and
lambda≥1 such that \(x_{i+\lambda}=x_i\) for every i≥mu. Before mu the states
are distinct and never recur. On the cycle, equal states have indices
congruent modulo lambda.

Brent fixes a reference and advances one runner. The block length P takes
values 1,2,4,8,…; the reference indices are P−1, hence 0,1,3,7,… .
When a block is exhausted without equality, copy the runner to the reference,
double P and clear the block offset. That copy calls no transition function.
Advance the runner before testing equality again: equality immediately after
a copy is not evidence of a completed cycle.

A block can succeed exactly when its reference is on the cycle and its
length is at least lambda. Its first successful runner offset is lambda.
Consequently, if P is the smallest power of two satisfying

$$
P>\mu,\qquad P\ge\lambda,
$$

period discovery makes exactly P−1+lambda calls. For the original map,
P=4, the successful reference is x_3, and the runner returns there at x_7.
The recorded table includes both no-call checkpoints and actual movements.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/brent_rho.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto next=dm::algorithms::rho_successors(3,4);
  const dm::algorithms::CycleFunction f=[&](std::uint64_t x){return next.at(x);};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::brent_cycle_trace(0,f);
  std::print("| event | reference index | runner index | reference state | runner state | P | offset | completed calls |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& row:trace.steps) if (row.phase==dm::algorithms::CyclePhase::detect)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",row.checkpoint?"copy + double":"advance",
        row.reference_index,row.runner_index,row.reference,row.runner,row.power,row.offset,row.evaluations);
  std::print("\n| detector | discovery | gap alignment | entry location | period measurement | total calls | equality tests |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const auto floyd=dm::algorithms::floyd_cycle_work(0,f);
  for (const auto& [name,work]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,dm::algorithms::CycleWork>>{{"Brent",trace.work},{"Floyd",floyd}})
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",name,work.detection_evaluations,
        work.alignment_evaluations,work.entry_evaluations,work.period_evaluations,work.evaluations,work.comparisons);
  const auto orbit=dm::algorithms::iterate_states(0,f,12);
  std::vector<double> index,state,tail_index,tail_state,cycle_index,cycle_state;
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<orbit.size();++i) {
    index.push_back(i);state.push_back(orbit[i]);
    if (i<trace.work.result.mu) {tail_index.push_back(i);tail_state.push_back(orbit[i]);}
    else {cycle_index.push_back(i);cycle_state.push_back(orbit[i]);}
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(index,state,"",dm::RGB{156,163,175},1.3)
      .points(tail_index,tail_state,"tail",dm::RGB{37,99,235},5)
      .points(cycle_index,cycle_state,"cycle",dm::RGB{22,163,74},5)
      .title("Original map: a three-state tail and period four")
      .x_label("orbit index i").y_label("state label x_i").y_limits(-.5,8)
      .size(900,480).save_svg("build/brent_rho.svg");
  std::print("\nFirst twelve states: {}.\n",orbit);
}
```
