// result_end
\caption(Checkpoint copies preserve the completed-call counter. The last discovery comparison is x_3=x_7=3, at offset four. State labels in the plot identify nodes; differences between labels have no geometric meaning.)
\alttext(The first three plotted points increase from zero to two. The subsequent state labels three, four, five and six repeat with period four, joined by a thin guide line.)

After discovering lambda, reset both pointers to x_0, advance the front pointer
lambda times, and then advance both together. The rear pointer first agrees
with the front at index mu: before then it is transient, and from mu onwards
the two indices differ by one full period. Alignment therefore costs lambda
calls and entry location costs 2mu calls. No additional pass is required to
return the entry state. The total is

$$
C_B=P-1+2\lambda+2\mu.
$$

Because P is less than twice max(mu+1,lambda), this is O(mu+lambda) transition
work. Ordinary detection and the work-summary API use O(1) auxiliary storage.
Recording the chronological states or materialising an orbit prefix requires
storage proportional to the number of retained records instead.

> Decimal digit squares: retain the original happy-number examples

For decimal digits \(d_j\), let \(f(n)=\sum_j d_j^2\). Positive n is happy when this
iteration reaches one; one then remains fixed. The native digit helper and
native classifier replace the original report-side implementation. The
reported entry comes directly from cycle recovery, without mu extra calls.
The classifier performs its own constant-storage detection; its calls are
separate from the displayed work summary.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| input | first digit-square sum | mu | lambda | cycle entry | happy | Brent calls |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (std::uint64_t n:{7,19,23,4,116,999,0,1}) {
    const auto work=dm::algorithms::brent_cycle_work(n,dm::algorithms::sum_squared_decimal_digits);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,dm::algorithms::sum_squared_decimal_digits(n),
        work.result.mu,work.result.lambda,work.entry,dm::algorithms::is_happy_number(n),work.evaluations);
  }
  std::print("\nCycle starting at four: {}.\n",
      dm::algorithms::iterate_states(4,dm::algorithms::sum_squared_decimal_digits,9));
}
```
