| 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | true | 2 |

Cycle starting at four: [4, 16, 37, 58, 89, 145, 42, 20, 4].
// result_end
\caption(The original positive examples are followed by zero and one as fixed-point controls. Inputs 116 and 999 first enter the unhappy cycle at 58 and 89, respectively, rather than at four.)

Every uint64 value has at most twenty decimal digits, so its first digit-square
sum is at most 20*81=1620. A positive value maps to a positive value. Enumerating
the maps from all positive inputs 1..1620 with an independent first-seen-state
oracle in the native tests verifies only two eventual cycles: the fixed point
one and the eight-cycle 4→16→37→58→89→145→42→20→4. Combined with the first-step
bound, this covers every positive uint64 input. Zero has its own fixed point
and is not happy. [OEIS A007770](https://oeis.org/A007770) records the decimal
happy-number definition and sequence.

> Controlled tails: exact counts against Floyd

Use generated rho maps with lambda=4 and mu=0..64. For the preserved Floyd
variant, let t be the first positive multiple of lambda at least mu:

$$
t=\lambda\max(1,\lceil\mu/\lambda\rceil),\qquad
C_F=3t+2\mu+\lambda.
$$

Each meeting-search round contributes three calls, including the initial
round's repeated f(x_0). Entry recovery contributes 2mu and period measurement
lambda. The programme checks both total-count formulas at every plotted
integer mu; the table selects values around block boundaries. These are
logical calls over all phases, without wall-clock timing or a random-map
average. The large jumps in Brent's curve occur when mu reaches a power of
two and the successful reference must move to the next block.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/brent_evaluations.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <bit>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::size_t lambda=4;
  std::vector<double> tail,brent,floyd;
  std::print("| mu | successful P | Brent discovery | Brent total | Floyd total |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t mu=0;mu<=64;++mu) {
    const auto next=dm::algorithms::rho_successors(mu,lambda);
    const dm::algorithms::CycleFunction f=[&](std::uint64_t x){return next.at(x);};
    const auto b=dm::algorithms::brent_cycle_work(0,f);
    const auto ff=dm::algorithms::floyd_cycle_work(0,f);
    const auto power=std::bit_ceil(std::max(mu+1,lambda));
    const auto t=lambda*std::max(std::size_t{1},(mu+lambda-1)/lambda);
    if (b.evaluations!=power-1+2*lambda+2*mu || ff.evaluations!=3*t+2*mu+lambda) return 1;
    tail.push_back(mu);brent.push_back(b.evaluations);floyd.push_back(ff.evaluations);
    if (mu<=4 || std::has_single_bit(mu) || std::has_single_bit(mu+1))
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",mu,power,b.detection_evaluations,b.evaluations,ff.evaluations);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(tail,brent,"Brent",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .lines(tail,floyd,"Floyd",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .title("Actual transition calls: fixed period four, varying tail")
      .x_label("tail length mu (integer samples)").y_label("completed f calls, including entry recovery")
      .y_limits(0,430).size(900,500).save_svg("build/brent_evaluations.svg");
  std::print("\nAll 65 generated maps match both exact count formulas.\n");
  try {
    dm::algorithms::brent_cycle_work(0,[](std::uint64_t x){return x+1;},{20});
    return 1;
  } catch (const std::runtime_error&) {
    std::print("A separate increasing-orbit demonstration stops at its 20-call budget.\n");
  }
}
```
