// result_end
\caption(All curves count the actual native detector's calls. Connected integer samples show block changes; they do not imply meaningful noninteger tail lengths. A smaller call count alone does not establish a faster runtime.)
\alttext(Brent's call-count curve rises gradually and jumps at doubling boundaries. Floyd's curve rises in four-step groups. Both grow linearly over tails from zero to sixty-four.)

The transition must be deterministic and callable for every encountered state.
The detector borrows the callback during its call; completed callback effects
are not undone when an exception propagates. Legacy two-argument detectors
remain available with their existing CycleResult layout. Diagnostic entry
points additionally accept an optional global completed-call budget. Zero
means unlimited; a positive budget throws before the next call would exceed
it, including during entry recovery. A call budget does not interrupt a
callback that itself fails to return. Arithmetic counters are checked before
wrap, and recording may also fail to allocate its owned vector.

> References and reproducibility

R. P. Brent (1980), *An improved Monte Carlo factorization algorithm*, BIT 20,
176–184, [doi:10.1007/BF01933190](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01933190).
The [author's publication page](https://maths-people.anu.edu.au/~brent/pub/pub051.html)
links the original paper. This report studies the deterministic doubling
variant's actual counts, including recovery, rather than treating the paper's
random-map average speedup as a measured result for these maps.

The inputs here are a fully specified seven-state graph, the original six
positive digit-map examples plus two controls, and generated rho maps. No
observational dataset or random seed is involved. The canonical cycle module
also has exhaustive small-map oracle tests, controlled period/tail tests,
callback-call-order checks, and limits tested in every phase. Each code block
is a complete independently executable programme; SVG files are rendered into
its local build directory and copied into figures by the report runner.
